     "main": "",
     "notes": [
      "Domestically, Australia delivers ongoing training and capability-building across digital and emerging technologies. This includes support for digital and AI skills, workforce transition and broader STEM participation. Relevant public initiatives include the National Skills Agreement, Fee-Free Technical and Further Education (TAFE), the Building Women's Careers Program, and the STEM Equity Monitor, which supports stronger evidence and accountability for diversity outcomes. \n\nFor example, the Building Women's Careers program aims to drive structural and cultural change to improve women's access to flexible, safe and inclusive training and work opportunities in male-dominated industries, including digital and technology, thereby strengthening women's participation in digital trade. It creates partnerships between government, industry, employers, women's services and training providers to ensure women with access to work opportunities in target industries. Seven digital and technology-focused projects are being funded under the program.\n\nAustralia's $50.3 million contribution to the Regional Trade for Development (RT4D) initiative responds to ASEAN-led priorities for trade policy capacity building and economic cooperation, to realise the full benefits of the Agreement Establishing the ASEAN-Australia-New Zealand Free Trade Area (AANZFTA) and Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP).\n\nRT4D has projects which provide training on digital entrepreneurship, e-commerce, and cross-border digital trade specifically targeting women. Some recent projects include:\n•\tCreative Sector Copyright Commercialization Programme to support MSME and Women in Cambodia\n•\tRural E-Commerce Empowerment Project in Cambodia\n•\tEmpowering Cambodian Disability Entrepreneurs through Intellectual Property Education and Brand Development for Trade\n•\tInclusive Cross-Border Trade Facilitation for Women Traders and SMEs under RCEP in Lao PDR\n•\tStrengthening SME Supply Chains by Empowering Women and Persons with Disabilities in Lao PDR \n•\tEmpowering women and people with disabilities through One District One Product development, packaging design, and e-commerce in Lao PDR\n•\tHandicraft and Employment Generation for Micro and Small Handicraft Female Entrepreneurs in Lao PDR ; and\n•     Promoting the utilisation of the RCEP, especially in digital trade, through better understanding and enhanced engagement of the private sector, micro, small and medium-sized enterprises (MSMEs), and women-led businesses in Vietnam.\n\nUNCTAD's eTrade for Women Initiative offers training to empower women digital entrepreneurs in developing and least developed countries to become more visible as role models and better heard by policymakers to foster change in the business enabling environment. Training is offered through eTrade for Women Masterclasses. Two Masterclasses have been held since 2024, one for South Asia participants (2024) and one for Southeast Asia and Pacific participants (2025)."
     ]
