      "Over the next three to five years, Chile's priority actions to strengthen women's participation in digital trade will focus on moving from broad digital awareness to effective technology adoption, market access and business growth.\nFirst, Chile will continue implementing targeted support programmes through \"Digitaliza tu Pyme\", using the \"Chequeo Digital\" self-assessment tool to segment MSMEs according to their digital maturity, sector, territory and business needs. This will allow more tailored pathways for women-led and women-owned firms, combining diagnosis, training, technical assistance, technology adoption and follow-up.\nSecond, Chile will expand digital and business skills initiatives. Training will prioritise practical capabilities for digital trade, including e-commerce, digital marketing, digital payments, cybersecurity, data use, customer management, logistics readiness and the use of emerging tools such as artificial intelligence. The objective is to help women entrepreneurs move from basic online presence to more productive and strategic use of digital technologies.\nThird, Chile will strengthen access to funding and investment opportunities for digital adoption. This includes linking firms to grants, subsidies, technical assistance and financing instruments that support the implementation of digital solutions, such as point-of-sale systems, inventory management, electronic invoicing, cloud-based tools, digital payment systems and cybersecurity solutions.\nFourth, Chile will support sector-specific initiatives in areas where women-led MSMEs have strong participation and potential, such as retail, tourism, food, creative industries, professional services, manufacturing and agribusiness. These sectors require differentiated support to address specific barriers related to scale, logistics, productive capacity, digital channels and market access.\nFifth, Chile will promote public-private partnerships. \"Digitaliza tu Pyme\" already works with public institutions and private partners to connect MSMEs with training, platforms, tools and digital solutions. This approach will be strengthened to improve outreach, relevance and implementation capacity.\nFinally, awareness-raising and outreach activities will remain important to reach women entrepreneurs across territories, especially micro and small firms that still face barriers to accessing digital markets."
