      "In Ecuador, women face the greatest challenges in digital trade within sectors that require higher levels of technological adoption, investment, innovation, digital capabilities, and access to finance.\n\nIn agriculture and agribusiness, barriers include unequal access to digital connectivity, digital technologies, market information systems, productivity-enhancing solutions, and digital commercialization channels, particularly in rural areas. Additional constraints include logistics, compliance with quality standards, business associations, and the adoption of digital tools needed to improve competitiveness.\n\nWithin the manufacturing sector, accelerating productive digital transformation remains a major challenge. Women-led MSMEs often require greater access to automation technologies, innovation support, specialized technical assistance, and investment to modernize production processes and compete effectively through digital channels.\n\nAlthough e-commerce provides significant growth opportunities, women entrepreneurs continue to face challenges related to digital platform utilization, digital marketing, online sales management, cybersecurity, logistics, digital payments, and customer relationship management. Addressing these barriers requires not only digital skills training but also sustained technical assistance throughout the digital transformation process.\n\nIn the banking and financial services sector, challenges remain regarding financial inclusion, access to credit, digital financial services, and electronic payment solutions. Expanding financial literacy and improving access to digital financial products are critical for increasing women's participation in the digital economy.\n\nThe energy sector also presents challenges due to women's underrepresentation in technology-intensive occupations, digital innovation, and advanced technology deployment, highlighting the need to strengthen women's participation in emerging digital industries.\n\nIn the tourism sector, particularly regarding ventures and organizations located in rural or community-based areas. The barriers relate to business capabilities, connectivity, access to digital tools, formalization, marketing, security, financing, the sustainability of ventures, and the strengthening of female leadership. These conditions limit the ability of women involved in tourism to effectively access digital markets or broader commercialization channels.\n\nTo address these barriers, Ecuador continues implementing policies and programmes under the Digital Transformation Public Policy, the National Agenda for Gender Equality 2025-2029, the National Digital Trade Strategy 2030, and national initiatives such as EmpoderaTech Ecuador, which aim to strengthen digital capabilities, promote technology adoption, and create an enabling environment for women entrepreneurs to participate more actively in domestic and international digital markets.\n"
