     "main": "",
     "notes": [
      "Ecuador provides broad-based support mechanisms that facilitate women's access to incubation programmes, business acceleration services, entrepreneurship support, and financing opportunities as part of the country's national innovation, entrepreneurship, and productive development policies.\n\nSupport is delivered through coordinated efforts involving multiple public institutions and ecosystem stakeholders. The Ministry of Production, Foreign Trade and Investments promotes programmes aimed at strengthening MSME competitiveness and business development, while organizations such as EPICO, the National Institute of Popular and Solidarity Economy (IEPS), universities, incubators, accelerators, and innovation centres provide mentoring, incubation, acceleration, and business advisory services accessible to women entrepreneurs.\n\nPublic financial institutions, including BanEcuador B.P. and the National Financial Corporation (CFN), offer financial products that support entrepreneurship and MSME development, subject to their institutional mandates and eligibility requirements. These efforts are complemented by initiatives implemented by international development organizations and private-sector partners to strengthen Ecuador's innovation and entrepreneurship ecosystem.\n\nWithin the digital transformation agenda, EmpoderaTech Ecuador contributes by preparing entrepreneurs to benefit from these opportunities through digital skills development, technology adoption, business capability enhancement, and productive digital transformation, enabling MSMEs to become better positioned to access incubation, acceleration, and investment programmes.\n\nAlthough most support mechanisms are universally accessible rather than women-specific, Ecuador promotes women's participation through inclusive policies, institutional coordination, and capacity-building initiatives designed to reduce gender gaps in entrepreneurship and innovation.\n\nAnd also under development, the \"Violet Tourism\" initiative recognizes equitable access to financing as one of its policy pillars. However, it currently lacks a direct mechanism for providing financing, seed capital, incubation, or acceleration services, or for establishing formal links with investor networks. Progress to date has focused on identifying this need, building capacity, and fostering collaboration with stakeholders who could help improve access to economic and financial opportunities for women involved in the tourism sector.\n"
     ]
