      "Monitoring of initiatives related to women's participation in the digital economy and digital trade is carried out mainly through planning tools, indicator tracking, and the collection of information on participants and activity results.\nUnder the National Strategy for Women Entrepreneurs (ENME), participating entities implement actions set out in annual work plans, whose progress is monitored by the MIMP as the lead institution. This monitoring helps track the implementation of activities, the number of participants reached, and the results reported by each entity.\nInformation from the Nominal Registry of Women Entrepreneurs and Business Owners is also used to profile the target population and provide disaggregated data on variables such as sex, age, location, female headship of household, and ethnic background. This information supports the targeting and monitoring of ENME interventions.\nSpecific initiatives also use additional monitoring mechanisms. For example, the Women's Economic Empowerment Support Centres (CAAEM) track both the mentoring process and participants, while the \"Thursdays for Women Entrepreneurs\" initiative records attendance and monitors training activities, including those related to digitalization, e-commerce, and digital tools.\nOverall, monitoring focuses mainly on tracking implementation, coverage, and intervention results. Impact evaluations are a separate mechanism and are not systematically conducted across all initiatives.\n"
