      "The project, entitled \"Women in Cross-Border Trade: A Case Study of Huaquillas-Aguas Verdes and Desaguadero\", was developed by Peru with the support of UN Trade and Development (UNCTAD) to identify the main barriers and challenges faced by women engaged in cross-border trade. The project focused on the Peru-Ecuador border, particularly the Huaquillas-Aguas Verdes area, and the Peru-Bolivia border, particularly Desaguadero, gathering information directly from women traders and other stakeholders involved in border operations. The findings highlighted challenges related to access to information on foreign trade regulations and procedures, border infrastructure and conditions, and other factors affecting women's participation in cross-border trade.\nFollowing the completion of the project, we are currently assessing the development of a roadmap to translate its findings into concrete actions. Among the areas being considered are capacity-building activities for women traders on foreign trade regulations and procedures; improvements to border control infrastructure and facilities, taking into account that women are among the regular users of these spaces and may have specific needs; and mechanisms to ensure that women's concerns and experiences are incorporated into regional working groups and other coordination mechanisms. The objective is to build on the evidence generated by the project and continue identifying practical trade facilitation measures that can improve the conditions for women engaged in cross-border trade.\n"
