    "Q1": {
     "options": [
      "Yes, explicitly included"
     ],
     "main": "",
     "notes": [
      "The Philippines explicitly incorporates women's inclusion in its digital economy, digital transformation, e-commerce, and financial inclusion strategies. The Philippine Development Plan (PDP) 2023-2028 identifies digital transformation as a key pillar for inclusive growth and highlights the need to expand opportunities for women, MSMEs, rural communities, and other underserved groups.\n\nThe Department of Trade and Industry (DTI) and the Department of Information and Communications Technology (DICT) have implemented programs that support women entrepreneurs through digital skills training, e-commerce adoption, and access to online markets. The Philippine E-Commerce Roadmap also encouraged greater participation of women-led MSMEs in online trade and digital entrepreneurship.\n\nIn digital finance, the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas (BSP) promotes financial inclusion through the National Strategy for Financial Inclusion and the Digital Payments Transformation Roadmap. These initiatives aim to increase access to digital banking and mobile payment systems, particularly for underserved sectors including women and women-led small businesses.\n\nExamples of initiatives include:\n\n1. DOST's Women Helping Women: Innovating Social Enterprises (WHWise) program, which supports women-led enterprises through capacity-building and market access;\n2. DTI's E-Taas ang Pinay and other broader MSME and innovation programs that encourage women's participation in the digital economy.\n\nDespite these efforts, challenges remain, particularly in internet affordability, digital skills, and access in rural areas, which continue to disproportionately affect women.\n\nReferences:\nhttps://pdp.depdev.gov.ph/\nhttps://pcieerd.dost.gov.ph/work-with-us/2026-pcieerd-whwise-program-call-for-proposal/\nhttps://pia.gov.ph/news/mindanao/nm/women-entrepreneurs-gain-digital-edge-through-dtis-e-taas-program/"
     ]
    },
    "Q2": {
     "options": [
      "Technological",
      "Organisational",
      "Economic",
      "Social",
      "Cultural"
     ],
     "main": "",
     "notes": [
      "Women's participation in digital trade and the digital economy in the Philippines is influenced by several interconnected dimensions of digitalization.\n\n1. Technological (Most Influential)\nAccess to affordable internet, digital devices, and digital skills is the biggest factor affecting women's participation. Many women, particularly in rural areas, face connectivity issues, limited access to laptops or smartphones, and lower levels of digital literacy. These barriers affect participation in e-commerce, online freelancing, digital finance, and remote work. Government initiatives such as DICT Philippines programs on digital literacy and DigitalJobsPH/SPARK aim to address these gaps.\n\n2. Economic\nEconomic constraints strongly affect women entrepreneurs and workers. Women-owned MSMEs often have limited access to financing, digital payment systems, logistics, and formal online marketplaces. The costs of internet services, technology adoption, and business formalization also limit participation. The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas Inclusive Finance Programs and DTI MSME support initiatives promote digital financial inclusion and e-commerce participation for women-led businesses.\n\n3. Social\nSocial factors such as unpaid care work, household responsibilities, and limited professional networks influence women's ability to engage in digital work and entrepreneurship. However, digital platforms also create flexible income opportunities that can help women balance work and family responsibilities. Programs supporting online freelancing and home-based digital businesses have become increasingly important for Filipino women.\n\n4. Organisational\nInstitutional support from government agencies, private companies, and civil society organizations shapes women's access to digital opportunities. Programs by the Department of Trade and Industry and DICT provide digital entrepreneurship training, e-commerce onboarding, and MSME support. Initiative from DOST such as Women Helping Women: Innovating Social Enterprises (WHWise) specifically support women entrepreneurs through mentorship and market access.\n\n5. Cultural\nCultural norms and gender stereotypes can discourage women from entering technology-related fields or leadership positions in the digital economy. In some communities, women may have less confidence or fewer opportunities to pursue technology and innovation careers. While this is gradually improving, cultural expectations still influence participation rates, especially in rural and conservative areas."
     ]
    },
