      "1. The Philippines has made digitalization of MSMEs a major policy priority. Through the Philippine E-Commerce Roadmap and DTI's e-commerce initiatives, businesses are encouraged to adopt online selling, digital marketing, and electronic payment systems.\n\n2. Developing digital competencies remains one of the Philippines' highest priorities. The Digital Workforce Competitiveness Act (RA 11927) institutionalizes programs to strengthen digital skills through collaboration among government, industry, and educational institutions.\n\n3. Supporting women entrepreneurs remains a central priority under the MSME Development Plan and the Magna Carta of Women. Government programs provide financing, mentoring, business development services, innovation support, and market access to help women establish and grow enterprises.\n\nSpecial attention is given to increasing women's participation in innovation-driven entrepreneurship, startup ecosystems, and high-value sectors. Financing institutions such as the Small Business Corporation (SB Corp), together with DTI programs and women-focused initiatives like the Great Women Project, contribute to strengthening women's entrepreneurial capabilities.\n\n4. Innovation is increasingly recognized as a driver of competitiveness in the digital economy. Through the Philippine Innovation Act (RA 11293), Innovative Startup Act (RA 11337), and Tatak Pinoy Act (RA 11981), the government seeks to foster innovation ecosystems, strengthen research commercialization, and support technology-based enterprises.\n\nWomen entrepreneurs are encouraged to adopt digital technologies, develop innovative business models, and participate in startup ecosystems through incubation programs, accelerators, innovation grants, and collaborative partnerships between government, industry, and academia. Strengthening women's participation in innovation contributes to greater economic diversification and resilience."
