      "1. Expanding Digital Skills Initiatives \n\nBuilding advanced digital and business skills is fundamental to increasing women's participation in digital trade. While the Philippines has made progress through programs such as DICT's DigitalJobsPH, TESDA digital skills training, and DTI's Kapatid Mentor ME (KMME), more women need training in high-value digital competencies, including e-commerce, digital marketing, artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, data analytics, and cross-border digital trade. Strengthening these skills will enable women to access better employment opportunities, improve business competitiveness, and participate in higher-value digital markets.\n\n2. Expanding Access to Funding and Investment Opportunities\n\nLimited access to finance remains one of the biggest constraints faced by women entrepreneurs. Expanding gender-responsive financing including grants, concessional loans, digital lending, venture capital, and startup financing would enable more women-owned MSMEs to adopt digital technologies, scale their businesses, and participate in domestic and international e-commerce. Improving financial inclusion is essential for translating digital skills into sustainable business growth.\n\n3. Implementing Targeted Support Programs\n\nBeyond general MSME assistance, the Philippines should expand targeted programs designed specifically for women entrepreneurs and workers. These should include mentoring, business incubation, export coaching, digital advisory services, and networking opportunities, particularly for women in rural communities, indigenous groups, and underserved sectors. Tailored support helps address the unique barriers women face and ensures that digital trade initiatives are more inclusive and effective."
