    "Q29": {
     "options": [],
     "main": "The Philippines has adopted a whole-of-government approach to promoting women's economic empowerment, MSME development, and digital transformation. While there is no standalone national policy specifically dedicated to women in digital trade, gender-inclusive measures are integrated across trade, digitalization, entrepreneurship, and innovation policies and programs.\n\nKey policy and legal frameworks include the Magna Carta of Women (Republic Act No. 9710), Philippine Development Plan 2023-2028, MSME Development Plan 2023-2028, Digital Workforce Competitiveness Act (Republic Act No. 11927), Philippine Innovation Act (Republic Act No. 11293), Innovative Startup Act (Republic Act No. 11337), Tatak Pinoy Act (Republic Act No. 11981), and the Gender Equality and Women's Empowerment (GEWE) Plan. These policies collectively promote digital transformation, innovation, entrepreneurship, financial inclusion, digital skills development, and women's participation in economic activities.\n\nGood practices include the Department of Trade and Industry's (DTI) Kapatid Mentor ME (KMME) Program, MSME Digitalization Program, OTOP Next Gen, and export promotion initiatives, which provide mentoring, digital entrepreneurship training, e-commerce capability building, and market access support. The Department of Information and Communications Technology's (DICT) DigitalJobsPH initiative strengthens digital skills and online employment opportunities, while the GREAT Women Project, implemented by the Philippine Commission on Women (PCW) in partnership with DTI and Global Affairs Canada, has strengthened women-owned MSMEs through enterprise development, market access, and business competitiveness.\n\nThe Philippines also actively participates in regional and international cooperation through ASEAN, APEC, and partnerships with organizations such as the International Trade Centre (ITC), United Nations Development Programme (UNDP), Asian Development Bank (ADB), and the World Bank to support MSME digitalization, digital skills, innovation, and women's economic empowerment.\n\nDespite these initiatives, opportunities remain to strengthen gender-responsive digital trade policies by developing dedicated programs for women in cross-border digital trade, improving access to advanced digital skills (including artificial intelligence and cybersecurity), expanding investment support for women-led digital enterprises, and establishing a more systematic framework for collecting sex-disaggregated data on women's participation in digital trade. These priorities align with the Philippines' ongoing efforts to build an inclusive, innovative, and digitally enabled economy.",
     "notes": []
    }
