     "main": "",
     "notes": [
      "Our answer does not identify specific sectors which face challenges, however it does provide some sector specific examples of problems identified.\n\nA recent Fawcett Society Report found that women make up only around 22% of the UK AI workforce.\n(https://www.fawcettsociety.org.uk/can-we-afford-to-automate-gender-inequality)\n \nFurther, social and cultural norms and barriers significantly shape participation. Gender stereotypes, care responsibilities, safety concerns, online harms, discrimination and low trust affect women's ability to access, use and benefit from digital technologies. Even where technological access exists, these factors limit women's engagement and progression in digital sectors. In the UK:\n\n·\t72% of women in tech experienced sexism at work, including lower pay (22%), questioned their skills and abilities (20%), assumed not to hold a technical role, and being interrupted or contributions credited to male colleagues. \n(https://www.fawcettsociety.org.uk/system-update-addressing-the-gender-gap-in-tech-report)\n\n·\t32% of women in UK STEM reported discrimination or harassment at work, up 7% year on year. \n(https://www.newscientist.com/article/2319755-discrimination-and-harassment-in-stem-industries-may-be-on-the-rise/)\n\n·\t1 in 4 women who have attended a tech conference report experiencing sexual harassment.  \n(https://www.ensono.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/Ensono_DataStudy_WomenInTech.pdf)\n"
     ]
